.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_TOTAL_CAPACITY 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_total_capacity \- report the number of elements a pool holds
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_mempool_total_capacity(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the total number of pool-owned elements mp holds, whether
currently in use or free. Dynamic fallback allocations are not counted;
see ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count(3) for those.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp                        Memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the number of elements the pool holds
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This is the elem_count passed to ccol_mempool_create(), or the element
count derived from the buffer passed to
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). It does not change over
the pool's lifetime.

A thread-safe pool large enough to maintain per-thread caches allocates
additional elements beyond this count, as a reserve that keeps the
advertised count obtainable by any thread regardless of how many entries
other threads are holding in their caches. That reserve is not reported
here, and it does not change the pool's memory footprint at run time:
ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes(3) reports the whole block, reserve
included, and it is allocated once when the pool is created.

The two ends of the count are not equally exact for such a pool. A thread
takes an entry from its own cache with no lock held and therefore with no
count to consult, so entries have to be committed to a cache before it is
known whether they will be needed. That decision favours the guarantee
above: a caller is never refused while another thread's cache holds
entries it was entitled to. In exchange, concurrent callers can briefly
hold a few entries more than this count at once, never more than the
physical slot count. A pool with no cache, which includes every pool
created with single_threaded = true and every pool built by
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(3), is exact in both
directions: allocation past this count fails, or falls back to the system
allocator.

See ccol_mempool_create(3) for how pool size affects performance.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Acquires the pool's lock, so it is a cold-path call and not intended for
use inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_used_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
